# HypeProof Studio — let OUR chat webview request the microphone.
#
# v3. FIVE HUNKS ACROSS THREE FILES. v1 shipped a blank app; v2 fixed that but the
# microphone stayed denied. Both failures are recorded here because the second one
# is not obvious from the code.
#
# WHY v2 WAS NOT ENOUGH — measured, not reasoned. Codex drove the rebuilt artifact
# from build run 34623231942 on a real Mac and read the actual frame policy chain:
#
#   top workbench document          microphone allowed = TRUE
#   outer workbench -> webview      allow="cross-origin-isolated; autoplay;
#     iframe                          local-network-access; clipboard-read;
#                                      clipboard-write"        <- NO microphone
#   webview shell document          microphone allowed = FALSE, allowlist []
#   inner shell -> content iframe   allow="... microphone;"    <- v2's hunk, present
#   chat content document           microphone allowed = FALSE, allowlist []
#   getUserMedia                    NotAllowedError: Permission denied
#   Chromium                        "microphone is not allowed in this document"
#
# Permissions Policy is delegated per level: an inner `allow` cannot re-grant what
# the embedder never received. v2 patched one level too deep. That same measurement
# is the evidence for the scoping below — an inner frame listing microphone while
# its parent is denied ends up with an EMPTY allowlist, so the effective grant is
# the intersection of the levels.
#
# THE FIVE HUNKS (no single one of them opens the microphone):
#   1 pre/index.html      CSP meta — the hash of this file's own inline script
#   2 pre/index.html      read the extensionId the shell was launched with
#   3 pre/index.html      inner iframe: delegate microphone, scoped to that id
#   4 webviewElement.ts   OUTER iframe: delegate microphone, scoped to the owning
#                         extension — this is what v2 was missing
#   5 app.ts              Electron 'media' permission for webview frames
#   + the OS — not patchable; NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is already present
#
# BLAST RADIUS — this is what changed from v2, read it before shipping. v2 keyed the
# inner delegation on `options.allowScripts`, i.e. EVERY script-enabled webview in
# the app. v3 keys BOTH levels on the owning extension id
# ('hypeproof.hypeproof-chat'), so:
#   - a webview belonging to any other extension gets upstream's allow list
#   - a webview with no owning extension gets nothing
#   - the future hypeproof-preview extension is out of scope by construction
#     (it has a different id and would need its own deliberate change)
# The id is available where it is read: `this.extension` is assigned from initInfo
# before `_createElement` runs, and the shell receives `extensionId` in its query
# string. Both were observed carrying 'hypeproof.hypeproof-chat' on the two frames
# in the shipped artifact, so this is not an assumption about plumbing.
# Residual: an extension that declared the same publisher.name would collide with
# the built-in. It is strictly narrower than v2, not a capability boundary.
#
# THE CSP HASH. `pre/index.html` carries the SHA-256 of its own inline script inside
# its own CSP meta (`script-src 'sha256-…'`). v1 edited a line INSIDE that script and
# left the hash alone, so Chromium refused to execute it, the shell never built its
# content iframe, and the chat and start pages came up BLANK — with build success,
# verify-branding pass and a normal artifact. Every edit in hunks 2 and 3 is inside
# that script, so the hash moves again:
#   v1/stock sha256-TaWGDzV7c9rUH2q/5ygOyYUHSyHIqBMYfucPh3lnKvU=
#   v2       sha256-F3cWHVJseSLSGcewJCgf4+lHqww+TPBT0lgjBiFyf7c=
#   v3       sha256-VQFZcn+4j5AR46C+MNBJ98l02nDWSQrtwWw+V6mBZmc=
#
# The v3 value was not guessed. The method was validated by reproducing the stock
# hash from the unmodified file first, and the repo's own gate
# (scripts/check-webview-csp-hash.py, a separate code path) independently agrees
# with it on the patched source. Its negative control was also run: the patched file
# carrying the old hash fails that gate.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: compute the hash from the script AFTER the hunk 2 and 3 edits, then
# write it into the CSP meta. The meta sits outside the script element so writing it
# does not perturb the hash again — asserted while generating this patch.
#
# ANY future edit inside that script invalidates this hash and the symptom is a blank
# webview with no visible cause. verify-branding.sh check 11 makes that a build
# failure instead of a silent ship.
#
# STILL NOT CLAIMED BY THIS PATCH: that the microphone works. Hunks 3 and 4 must both
# be present for the policy to reach the content document, and the OS prompt is after
# that. Only a real-device run settles it (H-01).
#
# Generated from microsoft/vscode at 560a9dba96f9 (upstream/stable.json tag 1.116.0).
